.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt SETSOCKOPT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm setsockopt ,
.Nm getsockopt
.Nd set or get socket options
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn setsockopt "int fd" "int level" "int optname" "const void *optval" "socklen_t optlen"
.Ft int
.Fn getsockopt "int fd" "int level" "int optname" "void *restrict optval" "socklen_t *restrict optlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions set or get option
.Fa optname
at protocol
.Fa level
on socket
.Fa fd .
For
.Fn setsockopt ,
.Fa optval
points to the value and
.Fa optlen
is its length in bytes.
For
.Fn getsockopt ,
.Fa optlen
points to the capacity of
.Fa optval ;
on success it receives the number of bytes copied.
.Pp
Except for
.Dv SO_BINDTODEVICE
and
.Dv IP_OPTIONS ,
results are four-byte integers, truncated to the caller's capacity.
Both output pointers must be nonnull even when that capacity is zero.
A truncated result reports its copied length, not four.
.Ss Socket options
The following options use
.Dv SOL_SOCKET :
.Bl -tag -width SO_BINDTODEVICE
.It Dv SO_REUSEADDR
Set or get a boolean stored on the socket.
A UDP bind may reuse a port only when every socket already bound to it
and the new socket have this option set, regardless of their owners.
TCP listener ports can be reused after the listener closes without
this option, but two live listeners cannot share a port.
.It Dv SO_BROADCAST
Set or get permission to send UDP to the limited or subnet broadcast
address.
.It Dv SO_TYPE
Get the socket's actual type.
Setting it succeeds without changing the type.
.It Dv SO_ERROR
Get and clear the pending error: a TCP connection's, or a connected
datagram socket's
.Er ECONNREFUSED
from a port unreachable.
The result is zero when no error is pending.
The error is cleared before output pointers are checked, so a failed
copy can consume it.
Setting this option has no effect.
.It Dv SO_BINDTODEVICE
The value is an interface-name string.
A nonempty name must identify a registered interface; an empty string
clears the setting.
A positive length is required.
The kernel copies at most 16 bytes into a zero-filled 16-byte buffer and
sets its byte 15 to zero.
Larger input buffers are accepted.
The kernel stores the named interface's index.
Getting it returns that interface's name when set, or an empty string
when clear, including the terminating
null byte if space permits.
A UDP, raw or ICMP datagram socket bound to an interface sends by it
alone: only its routes are looked up, and a destination none of them
covers is taken to be on its link, as on Linux.
An address of the machine's own is still reached through loopback.
The setting does not restrict what the socket receives, and a TCP
socket does not consult it.
.It Dv SO_RCVBUF
Set the bytes a datagram socket's receive queue may hold, as Linux does:
the value, at most 212992, is doubled to cover each datagram's record, and
is at least 2304.
Getting it returns the doubled value; the default is 212992.
A datagram that arrives with the budget spent is dropped.
.It Dv SO_KEEPALIVE
Set or get a boolean stored on the socket, which on a TCP socket turns on
the keepalive probes of
.Xr tcp 7 .
.It Dv SO_PASSCRED
Set or get whether a netlink socket's receives give each datagram's
sender's credentials as
.Dv SCM_CREDENTIALS
.Pq Xr netlink 7 ;
on a socket of another family other than
.Dv AF_UNIX
.Pq Xr unix 7
it is accepted, changes nothing and reads back 0.
.It Dv SO_TIMESTAMP , SO_TIMESTAMPNS
Set or get whether a UDP, raw or packet socket's receives give the time
each datagram was queued
.Pq Xr recv 2 ,
and an AF_UNIX datagram or sequenced-packet socket's the time each was sent
.Pq Xr unix 7 ;
SO_TIMESTAMP in microseconds and SO_TIMESTAMPNS in nanoseconds.
The old options, 29 and 35, give it in the caller's
.Vt struct timeval
or
.Vt struct timespec ;
the new ones, 63 and 64, in two 64-bit words.
As on Linux, turning one on turns the others off, turning any off turns
all off, and each reads back 1 only while it is the one on.
Other sockets accept them and give no timestamps.
.It Dv SO_RCVTIMEO , SO_SNDTIMEO
Accepted without effect on an Internet, packet or netlink socket.
It does not bound a wait, and getting it returns a zero
.Vt struct timeval ,
no limit, truncated to the supplied capacity.
The old options, 20 and 21, take the caller's
.Vt struct timeval
of longs, two 32-bit words from an i386 program on the x86-64 kernel;
the new ones, 66 and 67, take Linux's
.Vt struct __kernel_sock_timeval
of two 64-bit words in every ABI.
.El
.Pp
The
.Dv SO_REUSEADDR
and
.Dv SO_BROADCAST
values must have room for an integer.
Zero disables the option; any nonzero value enables it.
.Ss Protocol options
.Bl -tag -width PACKET_AUXDATA
.It Dv IPV6_V6ONLY
At level IPPROTO_IPV6, set or get whether an AF_INET6 socket excludes IPv4.
The default is zero, permitting mapped IPv4 peers.
Set this integer option before binding or opening a TCP control block;
changing it afterwards fails with EINVAL.
.It Dv IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS , IPV6_MULTICAST_HOPS , IPV6_TCLASS
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IPV6 ,
set or get the hop limit of an AF_INET6 socket's unicast and multicast
datagrams and the traffic class of its datagrams, integers from \-1
through 255.
\-1 gives the default: for the unicast hop limit, the one a router
advertised for the card the route takes, else 64, which is what reads
back; for the multicast hop limit, 1; for the class, 0.
UDP and raw ICMPv6 sends use them; TCP does not.
.It Dv IPV6_MULTICAST_IF
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IPV6 ,
set or get the index of the interface a datagram to a group leaves by
when its address has no scope id and the socket is bound to no
interface, or 0 for none.
The index must exist, and on a socket bound to an interface be that one.
.It Dv IPV6_MULTICAST_LOOP
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IPV6 ,
set or get whether a datagram this socket sends to a group comes back to
this machine's members of the group on the card it leaves by, 1, the
default, or 0.
A datagram that leaves in fragments does not come back.
.It Dv IPV6_JOIN_GROUP , IPV6_LEAVE_GROUP
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IPV6 ,
make an AF_INET6 datagram or raw socket a member of a group, or no
longer one.
The value is a
.Vt struct ipv6_mreq :
the group and an interface index.
A join with index 0 takes the interface the group's route takes; a leave
with index 0 takes the socket's first membership of the group.
A socket holds at most eight memberships, which it gives back when it is
closed, and the machine reports each group with MLD.
.Fn getsockopt
of either fails with
.Er ENOPROTOOPT .
.It Dv IPV6_RECVPKTINFO , IPV6_RECVHOPLIMIT , IPV6_RECVTCLASS
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IPV6 ,
set or get whether a
.Xr recvmsg 2
on an AF_INET6 UDP or raw socket gives the control messages
.Dv IPV6_PKTINFO ,
.Dv IPV6_HOPLIMIT
and
.Dv IPV6_TCLASS
of each datagram, as
.Xr recv 2
describes.
Zero clears the option and any other value sets it.
.It Dv ICMP6_FILTER
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_ICMPV6 ,
set or get the filter of a raw ICMPv6 socket, a
.Vt struct icmp6_filter
of 256 bits, one for each ICMPv6 type, a set bit blocking the type, as
.In netinet/icmp6.h
manipulates it.
A new socket passes every type.
.Fn setsockopt
takes at most the 32 bytes of the structure and leaves the bits past a
shorter value as they were;
.Fn getsockopt
gives at most 32.
.It Dv IP_TTL
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IP ,
set or get the IPv4 hop limit.
Setting it requires an integer from 1 through 255.
UDP and raw ICMP sends use the stored value.
.It Dv IP_RECVTTL
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IP ,
set or get whether a
.Xr recvmsg 2
on an IPv4 raw or ICMP datagram socket answers with the datagram's TTL,
a control message of level
.Dv IPPROTO_IP
and type
.Dv IP_TTL
holding an integer.
A UDP socket keeps no TTL of its datagrams and answers with none.
.It Dv IP_MTU_DISCOVER
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IP ,
set or get what an IPv4 UDP or raw send does with a datagram past its
path MTU, the interface's MTU or less for a destination a
fragmentation-needed report lowered in the last ten minutes, by Linux's
values:
.Dv IP_PMTUDISC_WANT ,
1, the default, sends it in fragments and one within the MTU with DF;
.Dv IP_PMTUDISC_DO ,
2, and
.Dv IP_PMTUDISC_PROBE ,
3, refuse it with
.Er EMSGSIZE ;
.Dv IP_PMTUDISC_DONT ,
0, sends every datagram without DF; and
.Dv IP_PMTUDISC_INTERFACE ,
4, and
.Dv IP_PMTUDISC_OMIT ,
5, send every datagram without DF, in fragments past the interface's MTU
whatever the path's.
.It Dv IP_HDRINCL
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IP ,
both calls fail with
.Er ENOPROTOOPT .
.It Dv IP_OPTIONS
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_IP ,
.Fn setsockopt
is accepted without effect, and
.Fn getsockopt
returns an empty value, a length of 0, since no socket keeps IP options,
as Linux reports for a socket that has none.
OpenSSH's server ends a connection whose socket reports any.
.It Dv TCP_NODELAY
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_TCP ,
accepted without effect; reads as integer zero.
The stack already sends promptly.
.It Dv TCP_KEEPIDLE , TCP_KEEPINTVL , TCP_KEEPCNT
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_TCP
on a stream socket, set or get the keepalive's idle seconds, 1 to 32767,
the seconds between probes, 1 to 32767, and the probes unanswered before
the connection is reset, 1 to 127.
Until set they read as the defaults of
.Pa /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_keepalive_time ,
.Pa tcp_keepalive_intvl
and
.Pa tcp_keepalive_probes .
.It Dv TCP_QUICKACK
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_TCP
on a stream socket, 0 holds acknowledgements for the answers to carry, and
any other value sends a held one at once; reads 0 while they are held so.
.It Dv TCP_USER_TIMEOUT
At level
.Dv IPPROTO_TCP
on a stream socket, set or get the milliseconds of unacknowledged data
after which the connection is given up, 0 for the default.
.Xr tcp 7
describes the five.
On a socket of another type they are accepted without effect and read as
integer zero.
.It Dv PACKET_AUXDATA
At level
.Dv SOL_PACKET ,
.Fn setsockopt
fails with
.Er ENOPROTOOPT .
The stack supplies no ancillary packet data.
.Fn getsockopt
returns integer zero.
.El
.Pp
Other options and levels are accepted without effect by
.Fn setsockopt
and return integer zero through
.Fn getsockopt .
Except for the interface-name and
.Dv PACKET_AUXDATA
cases, setting an option with a declared length of at least four
bytes reads the first integer even if the option is ignored; the length
is taken as a signed 32-bit value, so one above 2147483647 reads no
integer, and an option that needs one then gives
.Er EINVAL
while an ignored option still succeeds.
Shorter values are ignored unless the option explicitly requires an
integer.
.Pp
These operations are subcalls 14 and 15, respectively, of i386
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
system call 102.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm setsockopt
is call 54 and
.Nm getsockopt
55.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return zero on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOPROTOOPT]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A required option value or length cannot be read or written, a required
pointer is null, or the
.Fn socketcall
argument array cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The length for
.Dv SO_BINDTODEVICE
is nonpositive as a signed 32-bit value;
an
.Dv SO_REUSEADDR ,
.Dv SO_BROADCAST ,
.Dv SO_KEEPALIVE ,
.Dv SO_PASSCRED ,
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMP ,
.Dv SO_TIMESTAMPNS ,
.Dv SO_RCVBUF ,
.Dv IP_MTU_DISCOVER ,
.Dv IP_TTL ,
.Dv IP_RECVTTL
or TCP keepalive or user timeout value is shorter than an integer; the hop
limit is outside 1 through 255;
.Dv IP_MTU_DISCOVER
is outside 0 through 5; or a TCP keepalive value is outside its range, or
the user timeout negative.
An IPv6 option's value is shorter than an integer, or a
.Vt struct ipv6_mreq ;
a hop limit or class is outside \-1 through 255, or a loop other than 0 or
1;
.Dv IPV6_MULTICAST_IF
names an interface other than the one the socket is bound to; a join or a
leave names an address that is no group; or the length of
.Dv ICMP6_FILTER
is negative.
.It Bq Er EADDRINUSE
The socket has joined the group on that interface already.
.It Bq Er EADDRNOTAVAIL
The socket is no member of the group it leaves.
.It Bq Er ENOBUFS
The socket holds eight memberships, or the interface its sixteen groups.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
A nonempty
.Dv SO_BINDTODEVICE
name does not identify a registered interface, an interface index of
.Dv IPV6_MULTICAST_IF
or a join does not exist, or a join with index 0 finds no route; an IPv6
socket of a network namespace other than the first names no interface
.Pq Xr unshare 2 .
.It Bq Er ENOPROTOOPT
.Dv IP_HDRINCL
was requested,
.Fn setsockopt
was used for
.Dv PACKET_AUXDATA ,
an IPv6 option was given a socket that is not AF_INET6,
.Fn getsockopt
was used for a join or a leave, or
.Dv ICMP6_FILTER
was given a socket that is not an AF_INET6 raw one.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Dv ICMP6_FILTER
was given a raw socket of
.Dv IPPROTO_RAW .
.It Bq Er EPROTO
A join or a leave was given a stream socket.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
does not refer to a socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
.Xr ipv6 7 ,
.Xr tcp 7
